This project introduces the Caausal Exploration Toolbox (CEX), a set of tools
for exploring high-dimensional causal structures. Using the Python framework,
CEX leverages the Python Learning Library (PyPL) to apply various algorithms
for inferring causal networks. The package includes methods such as PC, TLIZ,
and HLC, which can be used to discover and orient edges in a causal network,
potentially enhancing results through score-based methods like PC-Score.

This study leverages lepton universality violations in $b 	o c$ transitions to speculate about the mechanisms underlying the rare process $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$. A comprehensive analysis is performed within the $U_1$ leptoquark model, focusing on limits imposed by $b 	o c$ lepton universality violations and the theoretical constraints on $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$. A full assessment of $B^0 	o K^{*0} 	au  ar{
u}$ and $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ branching ratios, along with the forward-backward asymmetry in $B^0 	o K^{*0} 	au  ar{
u}$ decays, is provided for various $U_1$ leptoquark mass values. Additionally, we examine the impact of these processes on the forward-backward asymmetry in $B^0 	o K^{*0} 	au  ar{
u}$ decays, presenting relevant results on minimum $U_1$ leptoquark mass and the optimal range for the relevant phases.
